    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d970">

                <pb facs="0985" n="973" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg973"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/9507: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-03-31T18:00:00+03:00">March 31, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 1—7:38 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>87. In conversation with our Military Attaché yesterday Von Papen<anchor n="78"
                        xml:id="d970-f1"/> made the statement that if Yugoslavia will not join the
                    new order peacefully Germany will compel her to do so by military force in order
                    to prevent the British and Americans forcing her into the other camp.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d970-f1" n="78" xml:id="d970-f1-content">
                <p>Franz von Papen, German Ambassador in Turkey.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
